# Without this, `COPY . .` pulls the host's node_modules and .next into the build context.
# Both are large, both are platform-specific, and .next would let a stale local build leak
# into the image and shadow the one the Dockerfile produces.
node_modules
.next
out

# The deps stage installs from package-lock.json; a host install is not the same tree.
npm-debug.log*

# Secrets never belong in a build context: anything copied in is recoverable from the
# image, even if a later layer deletes it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The E2E suite writes the key and recovery codes of its test administrator here (see ../.gitignore); the Dockerfile does `COPY . .`.
e2e/.auth/

# Generated by `next typegen` / `next build` on the host.
next-env.d.ts
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

.git
.gitignore
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Test files are not part of the image, and some of them import files that live outside this folder
# (scripts/fixtures at the repository root), which the build context does not contain. `next build` type-checks
# every *.ts(x) of the project, so a test file in the context fails the image with TS2307 on a clean clone.
# test/docker-context.test.ts keeps this list and the imports of the application code in line.
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test.tsx
